    <p>
      <mark>Review: the 2012 date is when it was posted online.</mark> The
      page ends with a citation of the author’s book
      <span lang="ru">«Очерки истории зарубежной черкесской диаспоры»</span>
      (<em>Essays on the History of the Foreign Circassian Diaspora</em>,
      Nalchik: El-Fa, 2007). The article is probably an excerpt from that book,
      so the text itself may date from 2007. The page doesn’t say so outright.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      The article is long and covers the whole history of the Circassian
      community in Syria, so I have <strong>not</strong> translated it in full.
      This page summarizes the passages about Amin Samkough in my own words and
      translates only a few short sentences. Everything is machine-assisted and
      should be checked by a Russian reader.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of,
      or a place where this article disagrees with other sources on this site.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>What the article covers</h2>
    <p>In order, the article describes (my summary):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        how Circassians came to Syria and how the community was divided by the
        French and British mandates after the Ottoman Empire broke up, with a
        table of Circassian and other North Caucasian villages and family counts
        from a French census in 1935
      </li>
      <li>
        the French mandate period (1920–1946), including how the French courted
        Circassian nobles and officers
      </li>
      <li>
        the Circassian national movement, led by a group around Amin Samkough,
        and its rivalry with a pro-Arab Circassian faction
      </li>
      <li>
        the Second World War in Syria, independence, and the community’s later
        position
      </li>
      <li>
        education, science, writers and artists, the Circassian Charitable
        Society (founded in Damascus in 1948), and renewed contact with the
        North Caucasus from the late 1950s onward
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="samkough">
    <h2>What it says about Amin Samkough</h2>

    <h3>Leader of the national movement</h3>
    <p>
      The article says that in the mid-1920s a group of intellectuals led by
      Samkough, whom it calls the group’s chairman, asked the League of Nations
      to recognise Circassians as a national minority and to grant autonomy in
      the Quneitra district. The group also sought Circassian seats in the
      Syrian parliament, official status for the Circassian language in
      Quneitra, and the right to open Circassian schools and newspapers. The
      French passed these requests on to the League. According to the article,
      the League’s Mandates Commission gave them little attention at its 11th
      session (late June to early July 1927). It records a repeat petition in
      1929 and a rejection of the autonomy proposal in June 1933, although the
      commission did find the requests about language study and national
      schools worth considering.
    </p>

    <h3>Education and career</h3>
    <p>
      He trained as an agronomist in Beirut, then finished a political economy
      course at the Sorbonne, and collected material on Circassian history while
      in Paris. On returning to Syria he was
      <mark>
        “appointed” (<span lang="ru">назначение</span>) chairman of the
        Quneitra municipality
      </mark>
      and began development work there (boreholes, roads, paving and greening).
      <mark>
        Review: the article says he was appointed. The biography page on this
        site and other sources say he was elected.
      </mark>
    </p>

    <h3>Parliament</h3>
    <p>
      A short passage, translated:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        В конце 1931 г. Амин Самгуг выдвинул свою кандидатуру в парламент
        Сирии, в котором было выделено два места для черкесской общины. Одержав
        победу в острой конкуренции с кандидатами от проарабской группировки,
        Самгуг был избран в парламент.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “At the end of 1931 Amin Samgug put forward his candidacy for the
          Syrian parliament, in which two seats had been set aside for the
          Circassian community. Having won a sharp contest against candidates
          from the pro-Arab faction, Samgug was elected to parliament.”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: the date. The article puts the election at the end of 1931. The
        biography page says 1931, and Arabic Wikipedia says he sat in 1931–1932.
        One Arabic-language local-history article I came across gives a date in
        March 1933. These may refer to different events (election versus taking
        a seat), so the dates should be checked against a primary source.
      </mark>
    </p>

    <h3>The society, the newspaper and the school</h3>
    <p>
      The article says the group founded the Circassian “Society for Education
      and Unity” in Quneitra in 1927. It gives the Circassian name as
      <span lang="kbd">«Адыгэ гъэсэн зыныгъэ хасэ»</span>. The society’s aims
      were to preserve the language and customs, to develop a national
      literature, and to seek autonomy. It published pamphlets and school
      textbooks in Circassian, using a Latin-based alphabet, and a newspaper.
      The newspaper section, translated:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        Общество также наладило выпуск газеты «Марж» («Mardj» — 1927-1932) на
        черкесском, арабском, турецком и французском языках. Главным редактором
        газеты стал Тарик Хагур.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “The society also set up publication of the newspaper ‘Marj’
          (‘Mardj’ — 1927–1932) in Circassian, Arabic, Turkish and French. Tarik
          Hagur became the newspaper’s editor-in-chief.”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      It then says the group opened a ten-year Circassian school in Quneitra in
      1932 for children from across the district. The school was paid for by
      donations and by income from shops owned by society members, the teachers
      wrote their own textbooks, and the curriculum included Circassian
      language, customs, songs, dances and sport. It names Samkough as one of
      the teachers, together with Ahmed Kat, Latif Ujukh, Charlotte Gutuk and
      Fakhri Khazhu of Quneitra, Yahya Sherkas of Manbij, Akif Khunag of Ayn
      Ziwan and Ismail Anzor of Raqqa.
    </p>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the school’s opening year.</strong> This article says
          1932. The article by Mirzoev and the biography page both say 1933.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the society’s name.</strong> This article calls it
          “Society for Education and Unity”. The Mirzoev article uses
          “Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture” and explains that
          “Society for Education and Unity” is the literal translation from
          Circassian. They seem to be the same organization.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the newspaper’s dates.</strong> The article says
          1927–1932. The front page on the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/marj-newspaper/">Marj page</a> is
          dated August 1931, which fits a run lasting at least until then, but
          I can’t confirm the end year.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The 1936 clash and its aftermath</h3>
    <p>
      The article describes a split in the Circassian community between a
      pro-French group and a pro-Arab group, with families and clans taking
      sides. It says that on 9 September 1936 an armed clash broke out between
      two youth groups: Circassian supporters of Samkough’s group and
      Dagestanis from a pro-Arab organization. There were deaths on both sides.
      One sentence, translated:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">Среди них брат Амина Самгуга.</p>
      <p><em>“Among them was Amin Samgug’s brother.”</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The Syrian government then used the incident as a pretext to accuse the
      society of collaborating with the French and banned it. The article says
      Samkough resigned as head of the Quneitra municipal council in protest.
    </p>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the brother.</strong> Nothing else on this site
          mentions that Samkough’s brother died in the clash. Please check it
          against another source before relying on it.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>who fought whom.</strong> This article says the other
          side was a group of Dagestanis from an organization it names
          <span lang="ru">«Кольчуга»</span> (“Chain Mail”). The Mirzoev article
          says the clash was between two Circassian youth groups and calls the
          pro-Arab organization the “Iron Shirts”. The names may be different
          renderings of the same group, but the Russian wording differs between
          the two sources.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>how he left office.</strong> Here he resigned in
          protest. The Mirzoev article and the biography page say he was
          dismissed.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>His writing</h3>
    <p>
      A late passage says that, besides his political work, Samkough studied
      Circassian history and folklore. It adds that in 1984, part of the
      surviving fragments of his unpublished book “Introduction to the History
      of the Circassians” were published in Damascus.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: the other pages on this site describe this as a book issued by
        his son after his death.
      </mark>
      The two descriptions are compatible, but the wording differs.
    </p>

    <h3>Life dates</h3>
    <p>
      The article gives his years as 1900–1953, matching the biography page.
      <mark>
        Review: the Arabic caption on one photograph gives 1903–1952, and the
        biography page says he died in May 1952.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
